# Project browser

`rocci-browser` is a fourth CLI: a product-blind host that registers project
directories, fuzzy-picks a **target**, and `load_url`s whatever HTTP origin an
adapter opened. It does not replace `rocci`, `rocdown`, or `rocci-okf`. Direct
`run` on those binaries still opens today's one-shot preview window.

:note[title: "Not `rocci browse`"] `rocci browse` is the component gallery over
`.rocci` files. `rocci-browser` is a project launcher. They share a word, not a
command surface.

## Run it

From a workspace checkout, build the host and the product CLIs, then either put
`target/debug` on `PATH` or keep the relative plugin rows in
`.rocci/browser.toml`:

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-browser
cargo run -q -p rocci-browser -- open knowledge --document plans/cli-entry-points --no-window --json
```

`--root` selects the directory that contains `.rocci/browser.toml`. With no
arguments, the host opens one persistent preview window.

## Keys

| Shortcut | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| **Cmd/Ctrl-P** | Host picker: filter registered targets. Enter opens the target home. Tab lists adapter documents, then Enter opens one. |
| **Cmd/Ctrl-K** | In-page Go to File (`goto.js`) on the visible origin. The host does not steal this. |
| **Cmd/Ctrl-F** | Find in the current preview page. |

The Dev panel iframe follows the current session's inspector URL. The host does
not parse inspect JSON.

## Registry

User projects live in `projects.json` under `ROCCI_BROWSER_DIR`, `$ROCCI_HOME/.rocci/browser`,
or `~/.rocci/browser`. Repo-local `.rocci/browser.toml` can add `[[plugin]]`
and `[[project]]` rows as data. The host does not hardcode target ids.

Switching targets reuses a still-warm server for the same root instead of
rebuilding on every hop. The previous child is grace-stopped after about 30s.

## Packaging

A signed `.app` for `rocci-browser` is **planned**. v1 is
`cargo run -p rocci-browser`. Product `rocci bundle` still packages a Roc app
gallery, not this host.
